## Withdrawals

The 44 files in the three binary-wrapped families above have been removed from `dist/`.
The two on-chain registrations that still rest on them (CVE_LOOKUP reg 1947, GAS_PRICE
reg 1535) are being replaced with our own builds.

Removing a file from the tree does not unpublish it. GitHub keeps serving any blob at a
commit-pinned raw URL and the registrations point at exactly those pinned URLs, so the
honest statement is that these bytes were published here and stay reachable through git
history. That is why they are listed rather than quietly deleted.
[`WITHDRAWN.json`](WITHDRAWN.json) keeps the full record, every file with its hashes and
its upstream, so the withdrawal is auditable rather than a gap.

If you are the author of any binary named above and want it handled differently, open an
issue on this repository.

## How to check any of this

Every claim above is derived from the bytes, not from memory:

```bash
python3 rev/provenance.py > PROVENANCE.json     # regenerate from dist/
python3 -c "import json; print(json.load(open('PROVENANCE.json'))['counts'])"
# {'own': 977, 'source_fork': 22, 'binary_wrap': 0}
```

`PROVENANCE.json` records, per file, the data-section digest that groups a build with
its base, whether our own `TELEGRAPH_INTENT` marker is present, whether the module was
post-processed with walrus (which means we did not compile it) and any upstream Rust
crate symbols left in the binary. A file we authored has our marker and no upstream
symbols. A fork carries the upstream's data section. A binary wrap has neither our
marker nor a base we ever compiled, which is why `binary_wrap` reads zero now: all 44
are out of the tree.

